{
  "$schema": "https://aka.ms/codetour-schema",
  "title": "3 - Clearance: failures, tradeoffs, and recovery",
  "nextTour": "4 - Clearance: the UI, tests, and your first change",
  "description": "After the normal payment path, reason about crashes, retries, offset gaps, locking, immutable records, operator recovery, retention, and observability. Read code and evidence; recovery exercises are predictions, not commands to alter data.",
  "steps": [
    {
      "file": "internal/outbox/publisher.go",
      "pattern": "^func \\(p \\*Publisher\\) PublishNext\\(",
      "title": "1. Locate the crash window before claiming reliability",
      "description": "**Prerequisite:** chapter 2's distinction between database commit, Kafka publication, and consumer processing. A failure can happen after one system succeeds but before another learns that it succeeded. Predict the remaining durable state in each case:\n\n| Failure point | What remains / what can happen |\n| --- | --- |\n| Creation rolls back | No newly committed payment, retry receipt, or outbox event |\n| Creation commits; HTTP response is lost | Retry key recovers the saved creation receipt |\n| Outbox claimed; worker dies before publishing | PROCESSING row can be reclaimed after five minutes |\n| Kafka accepts; MarkPublished fails | Reclaimed work may deliver a duplicate event |\n| Consumer DB commit succeeds; offset commit fails | Replay must find the existing processed-event record |\n\n`PublishNext` marks an event published only after the publisher reports success. Marking first would risk a permanent skipped event. Marking afterwards permits duplicates: that is the deliberate safety tradeoff at this boundary.\n\nA comment in `internal/postgres/outbox.go` says 'no double-publish'. Interpret that only as ordinary competing claims, not an end-to-end guarantee. A slow worker can also exceed the five-minute reclaim interval and overlap another worker. No lease renewal/fencing mechanism is shown here.\n\n**Check yourself:** Would adding more retries make sending to Kafka and updating PostgreSQL one atomic operation?\n\n**Answer — read after trying:** No. Retries help progress after transient failures; stable identity and consumer deduplication address duplicate effects. Attempts can also exhaust and require recovery."
    },
    {
      "file": "internal/consumer/loop.go",
      "pattern": "^func retry\\(",
      "title": "2. Read retry timing and cancellation precisely",
      "description": "A **transient** failure may clear on another attempt; a **poison message** consistently fails, for example because its JSON is malformed. `RunLoop` fetches a message, runs the handler up to `MaxAttempts` (default three), then invokes dead-letter handling if attempts fail. A dead letter is preserved failed work for investigation.\n\nInside `retry`, `fn func() error` is a function passed as a value. `for attempt := 1; ...; attempt++` counts attempts. `time.Duration(attempt) * baseDelay` is linear backoff, not exponential, and no random jitter is added. `select` waits for either cancellation through `ctx.Done()` or a timer. This permits prompt shutdown instead of an unconditional sleep.\n\nBenefit: temporary outages need not immediately become operator work; a bound prevents endless retries of the same message. Cost: retries increase latency and load. This implementation retries all handler errors alike and even waits after the last failed attempt when delay is positive; classify what the code actually does before describing a more sophisticated policy.\n\n`internal/consumer/loop_test.go` exercises retry exhaustion, successful dead-letter movement, and commit using test doubles.\n\n**Check yourself:** Would insufficient funds benefit from retrying the same already-finalized payment?\n\n**Answer — read after trying:** No. It is a business FAILED outcome committed by the ledger, not a transient handler error. A timer cannot create missing funds or reverse finalization."
    },
    {
      "file": "internal/consumer/loop.go",
      "pattern": "^func RunLoop\\(",
      "title": "3. Understand the known offset gap",
      "description": "An **offset commit** tells a consumer group how far it has progressed in one topic partition. `FetchMessage` retrieves work without automatically committing it. `CommitMessages` with a higher offset also advances past earlier offsets in that same partition; see [kafka-go explicit commits](https://github.com/segmentio/kafka-go#explicit-commits).\n\nNow inspect the `continue` after dead-letter movement fails. It returns to fetching, not to retrying that exact message. Consider messages A then B in one partition: A fails handling and durable dead-letter storage; the loop continues; B succeeds and its later offset commits. That commit can pass A even though A has no successful effect or durable failure record.\n\nThis is a source-based failure scenario, not a regression test demonstrated by the current suite. The comment claiming that not committing automatically retries A is too strong. `internal/consumer/loop_test.go` covers a successful DLQ move, not this two-message combined failure.\n\nA design alternative is to block that partition until A succeeds or is durably parked, or fail/restart the reader so replay can recover it. That protects unresolved work at the cost of progress during outages. A real fix needs tests for ordering, cancellation, and restart behavior.\n\n**Check yourself:** Is 'we never commit failed messages' sufficient to prove no work can be skipped?\n\n**Answer — read after trying:** No. A later commit in the same partition can advance past an unresolved earlier message. Database deduplication and safe offset advancement solve different problems."
    },
    {
      "file": "internal/deadletter/recorder.go",
      "pattern": "^func \\(r \\*Recorder\\) Move\\(",
      "title": "4. Preserve failed work before publishing its dead letter",
      "description": "`Recorder.Move` first saves a durable PostgreSQL record, then publishes a Kafka dead-letter message, then marks that publication. It preserves the original key, headers, bytes, topic, partition, and offset. `recordFromMessage` hashes the payload and creates a deterministic record ID from the consumer and source coordinates, so recording the same source failure can find the same record.\n\nDon't merge three different states in your mental model: an outbox row marked DEAD_LETTERED means publication attempts exhausted; a durable dead-letter record means consumer input failed; the Kafka dead-letter topic is an additional published copy. Their recovery paths differ.\n\nBenefit: the original input remains inspectable even if later publication fails. Cost: extra storage, an additional publish/update window, and sensitive payload retention. Error text is bounded and control characters are sanitized, but the stored payload itself remains exact bytes; bounded text is not comprehensive secret redaction.\n\n`internal/deadletter/recorder_test.go` checks preservation and errors from persistence, publication, and marking. Those component tests do not erase the consumer-loop gap in the preceding step.\n\n**Check yourself:** Can 'publish dead letter failed' mean the original bytes were nevertheless stored?\n\n**Answer — read after trying:** Yes. Persistence precedes publication. Determine which operation failed; if persistence itself failed, that durable recovery record does not exist."
    },
    {
      "file": "internal/operations/service.go",
      "pattern": "^func \\(s \\*Service\\) ReplayDeadLetter\\(",
      "title": "5. Treat recovery as another fallible operation",
      "description": "`cmd/clearance-admin/main.go` wires a trusted operations CLI to the store and broker. `ReplayDeadLetter` requires a reason, OPEN state, an unexpired replay window, and no detected prior processing for the event ID. It records a replay attempt, republishes the original key/headers/bytes to the source topic, then records the result.\n\nBenefit: recovery preserves identity and operator intent. Cost: publication and the final audit update are separate; a successful publish followed by failed bookkeeping leaves an uncertain outcome. REPUBLISHED means sent again, not successfully processed. An unchanged malformed payload will fail again unless the underlying cause is addressed.\n\n`RequeueOutbox` is a different action: it checks DEAD_LETTERED state, then returns the row to PENDING with an audit action in the database. It does not process the payment itself. The CLI is outside HTTP; static HTTP bearer scopes are not the CLI's access-control model. It relies on trusted access to database/broker configuration.\n\nRead `internal/operations/service_test.go` and `internal/postgres/operations_integration_test.go` for exact replay, audit, failure, and competing replay-claim checks. Do not run replay against arbitrary records as a learning exercise.\n\n**Check yourself:** Does a recorded operator reason prove which authenticated human performed the action?\n\n**Answer — read after trying:** No. A reason records intent; these fields are not an identity/RBAC system. Also, a successful replay command still requires checking the eventual business outcome."
    },
    {
      "file": "internal/maintenance/processed_events.go",
      "pattern": "^func NewProcessedEventsService\\(",
      "title": "6. Connect retention to the deduplication promise",
      "description": "Processed-event receipts take storage indefinitely unless pruned. **Retention** is how long the system keeps them; the **replay window** limits how old a failure may be when an operator republishes it. Defaults are 30 days of retention and a 14-day replay window. The constructor rejects retention shorter than replay; equality is allowed by the comparison.\n\n`Preview` computes a cutoff without deleting records. `Prune` asks the store to remove a bounded batch; the default is 1,000, capped at 10,000. The PostgreSQL implementation uses last_seen_at, protects receipts associated with open/recent dead letters, coordinates maintenance with an advisory lock, and records the action. There is no built-in periodic scheduler here.\n\nBenefit: bounded batches control storage growth and limit each maintenance operation. Cost: deleting receipts also deletes that historical proof of processing. Supporting very old replays would require longer retention or another durable identity strategy. The configured windows govern supported recovery; they do not make arbitrary old broker replays safe.\n\nRead `internal/maintenance/processed_events_test.go` for invalid-window and batch behavior. A preview result is evidence about current candidates, not permission to delete production history.\n\n**Check yourself:** After a receipt is pruned, can you promise the same event will be recognized forever?\n\n**Answer — read after trying:** No. Effectively-once effects depend on retained identity and the supported replay assumptions. Other business guards may reject some repeats, but that is not universal deduplication."
    },
    {
      "file": "internal/postgres/query.go",
      "pattern": "^func \\(s \\*Store\\) ListTransactions\\(",
      "title": "7. Learn operator queries and cursor pagination",
      "description": "The operator list API supports account, status, kind, limit, and cursor filters. `internal/transaction/query.go` validates these before calling SQL. A **cursor** encodes the last row's `(created_at, id)` position. The SQL orders descending by those two values and asks for rows with smaller tuples. The ID resolves equal timestamps.\n\n`limit $6` receives `filter.Limit+1`: the extra row tells the service whether a next page exists. `($2 = '' or status = $2)` makes an empty status filter optional. `rows.Next()` advances, `rows.Scan(...)` reads the current row, `defer rows.Close()` releases resources, and `rows.Err()` catches iteration errors after the loop. Omitting that final error check could return incomplete data as success.\n\nBenefit over large OFFSET values: continue from a stable ordering position rather than skipping a growing number of rows. Costs: cursor encoding/validation and no convenient jump to arbitrary page 500. This is not a frozen snapshot across requests; concurrent data changes can affect later pages.\n\n`internal/httpapi/transactions.go` requires the operator credential for listing. `internal/transaction/query_test.go` checks validation/pagination; the funding/query integration test checks real SQL.\n\n**Check yourself:** Why isn't created_at alone enough for pagination?\n\n**Answer — read after trying:** Multiple transactions can share a timestamp. Adding the ID makes the ordering position unambiguous."
    },
    {
      "file": "migrations/001_init.sql",
      "pattern": "^create trigger trg_prevent_ledger_entry_update",
      "title": "8. Separate immutable history from balanced writes",
      "description": "The trigger rejects ordinary UPDATE and DELETE of ledger rows. A **trigger** is database logic invoked by specified operations. The table also has `unique(transaction_id, account_id)` to reject a duplicate account entry for that transaction.\n\nThe payment path appends an account debit and clearing credit; deposits append an account credit and external-settlement debit. In our example: `+50000` funding, then `-12550` payment leaves `37450` cents for acct_123. Each supported movement has equal/opposite entries in one currency.\n\nBenefit: history remains auditable and balances can be recomputed. Costs: more rows, growth over time, and balance queries that sum history. Application code constructs balanced pairs; no general database constraint here enforces a zero sum across arbitrary inserts. Nor does this trigger prove immutability against privileged database administration or every possible operation.\n\nA correction would normally be a new compensating record rather than editing history; a correction/reversal API is not implemented by this observation. The existing unit test named CreatesImmutableLedgerEntries does not actually attempt database UPDATE/DELETE. Chapter 4 distinguishes stronger rollback evidence.\n\n**Check yourself:** Can a trigger rejecting UPDATE/DELETE prove every INSERT is balanced?\n\n**Answer — read after trying:** No. Immutability and balanced bookkeeping are different properties with different enforcement and test requirements."
    },
    {
      "file": "internal/metrics/metrics.go",
      "pattern": "^type Registry struct",
      "title": "9. Observe progress without mistaking liveness for success",
      "description": "**Logs** describe individual events; **metrics** aggregate measurements; **traces** connect spans of work across a distributed operation. This repo has structured logs, correlation IDs, and optional Prometheus metrics, but not a complete distributed tracing system.\n\nA **counter** counts events, a **gauge** measures a value that can rise/fall (such as backlog), and a **histogram** groups measurements such as request duration into buckets. Metric labels create separate series for combinations like service, route, and result. Keeping labels low-cardinality means avoiding millions of distinct values such as one series per transaction ID.\n\n`METRICS_ENABLED=true` exposes `/metrics`. Files in `deploy/` configure Prometheus collection and Grafana dashboards. `/healthz` reports process liveness; it does not exercise the whole payment pipeline. A live HTTP server with a growing outbox backlog may still be accepting work that cannot finish.\n\nBenefit: measurements locate stalls without reproducing every request. Cost: collection/storage work and careful label design. Correlation IDs help locate logs but must not be confused with authorization or deduplication keys.\n\n**Check yourself:** A receipt is stuck PENDING while /healthz returns OK. What evidence would you seek?\n\n**Answer — read after trying:** The transaction row, pending/dead outbox work, consumer errors or dead letters, and broker availability. Health alone does not identify whether publication, risk, or ledger processing stalled."
    },
    {
      "file": "docs/architecture.md",
      "pattern": "^## Operational Boundaries",
      "title": "10. Defend the decisions and choose simpler alternatives",
      "description": "Explain a design as a problem, the implemented mechanism, its benefit, and its cost. The README supplies the learning objective; these comparisons are engineering analysis of the code.\n\n| Current choice | Benefit | Cost / simpler alternative |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| Separate asynchronous stages | Durable handoff and independent workers | Lag/recovery/contracts; one synchronous service is simpler for this rule |\n| Explicit SQL and shared PostgreSQL | Visible atomic boundaries | Manual mapping and shared schema coupling; independent stores need another consistency design |\n| Transactional outbox/inbox | Recoverable intent and repeat-safe committed effects | Tables, polling, retention; direct publication is simpler but has a failure gap |\n| Integer cents and append-only ledger | Exact unit arithmetic and inspectable history | Currency/range policy and growing history; a mutable balance is simpler but loses that history |\n| Static bearer credentials | Easy local setup and distinct operation scopes | No user/account identity model |\n\nDon't choose complexity by vocabulary alone. For a small synchronous app with no asynchronous consumers, an outbox may add little benefit. For this repo's reliability learning objective, exposing those failure boundaries is the point.\n\nContinue to chapter 4 to see how the UI observes them and which tests establish which guarantees.\n\n**Check yourself:** Explain the system to a new engineer in four sentences, including one limitation.\n\n**Answer — read after trying:** An acceptable explanation names durable HTTP acceptance, risk/ledger workers, atomic database writes plus duplicate-safe event handling, and a specific limit such as the offset gap, static account authorization, or single-broker topology."
    }
  ]
}
